Now implement. First `ChildScope.java`. Design:

```java
package dev.pathland.view;

import java.util.Collection;

/**
 * The mutable collector passed to a block-form {@code children(...)} call
 * (spec DSL.md §2):
 *
 * <pre>{@code
 * VStack.make(v -> v.spacing(8)).children(content -> {
 *     content.add(Text.make("a"));
 *     content.add(Text.make("b"));
 * });
 * }</pre>
 *
 * <p>Each {@link #add(View...)} appends in call order; the collected children are
 * handed to the same {@link ViewBuilder#children(View...)} path as the varargs
 * form, so the two produce byte-identical opcode output.
 */
@FunctionalInterface
public interface ChildScope {

    /** Append one or more children in call order. */
    void add(View... children);

    /** Append every view in {@code children} in iteration order. */
    default void addAll(Collection<? extends View> children) {
        add(children.toArray(new View[0]));
    }
}
```

Wait: `@FunctionalInterface` with a default method is fine (one abstract method).

But an implementing anonymous class in ViewBuilder will need `add` and inherit `addAll`. Good.

Now `ViewBuilder`:

```java
/** Supply the content (content-bearing views only). */
public ViewBuilder<V, C> children(View... children) { ... }

/**
 * Supply the content from a block (spec DSL.md §2) — the same operation as
 * {@link #children(View...)}, with each {@link ChildScope#add(View...)} appended
 * in call order. Pure sugar: the collected children take the same path, so both
 * forms emit identical opcodes.
 */
public ViewBuilder<V, C> children(Consumer<ChildScope> content) {
    ChildScope scope = children -> this.children(children);
    content.accept(scope);
    return this;
}
```

Hmm, simpler:
```java
public ViewBuilder<V, C> children(Consumer<ChildScope> content) {
    content.accept(this::children);
    return this;
}
```
Does `this::children` match `void add(View...)`? `this.children(View...)` returns `ViewBuilder<V,C>`, but a method reference to a value-returning method can be used where a `void` functional method is expected (the return value is discarded). Yes, Java allows adaptation: "If the function type's result is void, the method reference is compatible if the referenced method's return type can be discarded." Actually, for method references, a void-returning functional interface is compatible with a value-returning method. Yes (e.g., `Consumer<String> c = list::add;` where `add` returns boolean). So `this::children` works and is elegant. But `ChildScope` also has `addAll` default; method reference just implements `add`. Fine.

Let me use `content.accept(this::children);` — concise. But is it clear? Maybe. Add Javadoc.

Alternatively:
```java
ChildScope scope = this::children;
content.accept(scope);
return this;
```
I'll use the explicit variable for readability.

