Potential issue: `FitScope` as a `@FunctionalInterface` with abstract `add(View, float)` and default `add(View)`/`add(Fit)`/`addAll`. That's fine. But maybe the primary abstract method should be `add(Fit)` to allow `fits.add(Fit.of(...))`? Then convenience `add(view)`/`add(view, minWidth)` default. Which is cleaner? 
```java
public interface FitScope {
    void add(Fit fit);
    default void add(View view) { add(Fit.of(view)); }
    default void add(View view, float minWidth) { add(Fit.of(view, minWidth)); }
    default void addAll(Collection<? extends Fit> fits) { ... }
}
```
This is clean: the fundamental unit is `Fit`; shorthand for view cases. But `@FunctionalInterface` abstract `add(Fit)` means a lambda can't directly be a scope? The scope is an object, not a lambda. `make(Consumer<FitScope>)` takes a lambda whose parameter is `FitScope`. The `FitScope` implementation is anonymous/private. Fine.
Alternatively, `@FunctionalInterface` abstract `add(View, float)` and defaults for others. Either. I prefer abstract `void add(Fit fit)` plus defaults, mirroring `ChildScope.add(View)`. But `add(View, float)` is more ergonomic. Let's define all three as default except one abstract. The plan can say the exact shape.

Maybe call it `FitScope` and implement:
```java
@FunctionalInterface
public interface FitScope {
    void add(Fit fit);
    default void add(View view) { add(Fit.of(view)); }
    default void add(View view, float minWidth) { add(Fit.of(view, minWidth)); }
    default void addAll(Collection<? extends Fit> fits) { fits.forEach(this::add); }
}
```
Then usage:
```java
SizeThatFits.make(fits -> {
    fits.add(wideRow, 1024f);
    fits.add(compactRow);
});
```
This reads well. The `add(Fit)` form still works. Good.

Now, `SizeThatFits.make(Consumer<FitScope>)`:
```java
public static View make(Consumer<FitScope> content) {
    List<Fit> fits = new ArrayList<>();
    content.accept(fits::add);
    return slot(fits.toArray(new Fit[0]));
